//! check: fail(E1010)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.region.escape, mem.region.account.1, mem.region.intra.2
//!
//! s160 (wolf-lang#191, with #321) — the FREE producer. `str_from_utf8`
//! copies the accepted bytes into the ambient region ("an ordinary
//! owned `str`, borrowing nothing from the caller's list", as its
//! runtime shim has always said), so it is an allocation site like
//! `repeat` or `+`. The row on its type (`str ! {utf8}`) is why the
//! mem tier could not see it by asking whether the result is `str`.
//!
//! This witness exists because #191 made it matter. While `str`
//! materialization landed in the process root, the escape was a leak
//! and printed fine; now that the bytes charge `scratch` and are freed
//! wholesale with it, the same program would read freed memory. The
//! refusal is what makes closing that seam safe.

fn build() -> str {
    region scratch {
        let b = "hi".bytes()
        str_from_utf8(b) else "?"   // ERROR E1010: built in scratch
    }
}

fn main() -> !int {
    print("{build()}\n")
    0
}
